  clock: 8  # cc46
  nonce: cc46  # cc46
  message: "RETRACTION — MY \"STRIKE RUST FROM SCOPE\" RECOMMENDATION WAS WRONG, ON THE\nOWNER'S CHALLENGE THAT IT NARROWED A GENERAL REQUIREMENT. It did, and the reason\nI gave for it does not hold. Corrected here rather than left standing.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. I cited WI-gihos as already answering rust. WI-gihos rules on\nwhere a catalogue ROW may be KEYED -- it forbids a static `BufReader` row because\none would be majority-wrong (8 of ~10 sites in `rage` wrap an in-memory slice).\nIt says nothing about ARGUMENT INFERENCE, and its own measurement is arguably\nevidence FOR inference, since inference is exactly what classifies those 8\ncorrectly as `in_memory`/no-crossing. A ruling against a row is not a ruling\nagainst the mechanism this item proposes.\n\nI also nearly assumed \"the wrapped constructor is rowed, therefore the flow is\nfound\". I had a counterexample IN HAND and did not apply it: c rows `stdio.stdin`\nas ipc_recv and STILL loses the flow, because the crossing lands on the FILE node\nwith empty entry_points (WI-pimun). Rowed does not imply found.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, with positive evidence rather than an assumption. Four\nfunctions, one cold run through the production CLI, control in the same run\n(artifact: lipis_third_deliverable_08312026/, rust3 probe):\n\n    control_direct_stdin   stdin() read directly        -> violated  (CONTROL LIVE)\n    from_stdin_wrapped     BufReader::new(stdin())      -> violated  <== FOUND\n    from_file_wrapped      BufReader::new(File::open)   -> no row    (correct)\n    from_memory            BufReader::new(&buf[..])     -> no row    (correct)\n\nAll four outcomes are already right. The source is minted at the WRAPPED\nconstructor and the wrapper is transparent to the flow. So rust SATISFIES this\nrequirement today -- which is a different statement from \"the requirement does\nnot apply to rust\", and only the second one narrows anything. The requirement\nstays general across all four languages; rust is the language where it is\nalready met, and that is now recorded with evidence instead of asserted.\n\nSCOPE OF THAT EVIDENCE, stated rather than implied: the probe has the wrapper and\nits wrapped constructor in the SAME function. A wrapper returned from one\nfunction and read in another is not covered by this run.\n\nMY FIRST RUST PROBE'S CONTROL WAS DEAD AND I ALMOST REPORTED ITS ZEROS. It used\n`std::io::stdin()`, which is not detected; every case read as \"no finding\",\nwhich would have looked like confirmation of exactly the wrong conclusion. The\nre-run uses the bare imported `stdin()`, which is detected, so the control fires.\nA gate that never fires proves nothing.\n\nAND THAT DEAD CONTROL WAS A REAL DEFECT, now filed as INV-zuvib (P1): a grouped\n`use std::fs::{read_to_string, File};` registers NO import aliases, so\n`File::open` lands as `rust:external:0-0:File::open:unresolved` and matches no\ncatalogue row, while the single-import form of the identical call resolves.\n97450 grouped use-lists across 121 rust repositories, 37.9% of all rust `use`\nstatements. It is not confined to io names.\n\nNET EFFECT ON THIS ITEM: scope UNCHANGED and still general -- c, java, haskell,\nrust. c is the live gap (measured, with a control). rust is met. java (448 sites,\n33 repos) and haskell (28 sites, 8 repos) are unmeasured, and I should not\npronounce on them the way I nearly pronounced on rust."  # cc46
- op: discuss  # cd0b
  at: '2026-09-01T04:24:11Z'  # cd0b
  by: agent  # cd0b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cd0b
  clock: 9  # cd0b
  nonce: cd0b  # cd0b
  message: "THIRD DELIVERABLE SHIPPED FOR c AND haskell. ITEM STAYS OPEN: java is measured,\nblocked and NOT shipped, and the item's title names all four languages.\nArtifact: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lipis_stream_argument_09012026/ (README\nfirst -- it carries ten traps).\n\nALL FOUR LANGUAGES ARE NOW MEASURED, each with a LIVE control in one cold run.\nThe previous entry warned against pronouncing on java and haskell the way I\nnearly pronounced on rust; this is that measurement rather than an assertion.\n\n  c        LIVE GAP   scanf violates / fgets(buf,n,stdin) silent   -> FIXED\n  haskell  LIVE GAP   readProcess violates / hGetLine stdin silent -> FIXED\n  rust     ALREADY MET (previous entry, rust3 probe)\n  java     LIVE GAP, NOT FIXED -- different shape, two blockers filed\n\nTHE DIRECTION IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE GO DELIVERABLE'S, which changes what a\nclean A/B means. go.yaml rowed the wrapper ipc_recv, which MINTS, so a wrong\nargument invented a source and the fix REMOVED rows. c.yaml and haskell.yaml\nrow their stream-takers fs_read, which mints nothing, so a wrong argument\nMISSES one. This ADDS rows and must be judged as new surface: removed=0 is the\nsuccess condition here, not added=0.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. Both families are declared under fs_read AND ipc_recv with\nboundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable, and `_narrow_by_target_kind` drops the\nlosing row using the stamped io_target_kind. It NARROWS on every path of\nlookup_with_module -- including the short-name fallback, where c's fgets\nactually lands and where the mode seam was once inert -- because it has to\nCOMPOSE with select_by_mode. The discrimination rule is \"two or more READ\nboundaries\" and earns its shape by what it excludes: builtins.open is\nfs_read + fs_write, fs_write is not a read boundary, so the mode seam keeps it\nand INV-rusof's fix stands. bufio.NewScanner is a single row, so the go\nbehaviour from deliverable 2 is unchanged.\n\nAN ABSTENTION RESOLVES TO THE FIRST-DECLARED ROW. A parameter, a popen handle,\nor a collapsed edge whose sites disagree classifies exactly as before, because\nfs_read is declared first in both catalogues. Declaration order is therefore\nload-bearing and is pinned by a test against the shipped YAML rather than left\nfor a reviewer to notice. The quantifier is ALL, not ANY -- the deliberate\nmirror of _source_call_can_mint_taint, where ANY is right because that gate\nREMOVES findings.\n\n*** THE CATALOGUE CHANGE ALONE WAS WRONG, AND ONLY READ-BACK CAUGHT IT. ***\nfs_read never becomes a TaintSource (it is absent from AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP),\nso after dual-declaring, the surviving ipc_recv row matched fgets BY NAME at\nevery call site -- including the file reads io-boundaries had just correctly\ntagged fs_read. One fact, two homes, second silently winning. Measured on the\nc repro: fgets over a popen handle minted untrusted_input while the same edge\nwas tagged fs_read. TaintSource now carries requires_target_kind, the exact\nmirror of the sink side's requires_mode since INV-rusof, and the two gates sit\ntogether in _match_propagation_entry.\n\nI ALSO WROTE AND DELETED A `select_by_target_kind`. It could have no production\ncaller without breaking composition with the mode seam, which is WI-famig's\ndefect -- a mechanism with no live consumer -- and its own uncovered line is\nwhat exposed it. Recorded so nobody re-adds it for symmetry with select_by_mode.\n\nCOHORT A/B, 8 repositories, BOTH ARMS COLD (each run asserts its own \"No cached\nresults found\"), baseline = the tree immediately before this commit and\nverified as baseline by its own control (the c repro shows only scanf):\n\n    added 2   removed 0   verdict-category moves 0\n\n*** BOTH ADDED ROWS WERE READ BACK AGAINST SOURCE AND BOTH ARE TRUE. ***\n  redis deps/linenoise/linenoise.c:1201   fgets(buf, LINENOISE_MAX_LINE, stdin)\n      reads a line from the terminal or a pipe and strdups it to the caller.\n  redis deps/lua/src/ldblib.c:306         fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin)\n      reads a Lua debug command from stdin and hands it to luaL_loadbuffer +\n      lua_pcall -- untrusted input reaching code execution.\n\nPRESENCE IS NOT FINDINGS, and the two numbers are reported separately because\ncorrect-and-inert and broken look identical at the finding level. Stamp census\non the same tree: redis carries 22 host_path + 3 std_stream c stamps, pandoc\n1 host_path + 1 std_stream haskell stamps. MOST STAMPS CORRECTLY DO NOT MINT --\nthat is the conservative default doing the work, not the mechanism failing.\nHASKELL MOVED ZERO ROWS on this cohort; its producer fires on pandoc, but no\nhGetLine stdin in these repos reaches a host_fs sink.\n\nA SECOND FIX CAME OUT OF THE COVERAGE GATE, not out of reading the grammar: a\nhaskell definition WITH parameters is a `function` node, not a `bind`, so\nsearching only `bind` abstained on every parameterised function -- the COMMON\nshape, since a Handle is usually opened from a path the function was handed.\n`f p = do { h <- openFile p ReadMode; hGetLine h }` now resolves to host_path.\n\nWHAT REMAINS ON THIS ITEM -- JAVA, and it is a genuinely different shape.\nc and haskell put the stream in an argument OF THE ROWED CALL. java puts it in\nan argument of the CONSTRUCTOR and rows a METHOD on the resulting object\n(`new Scanner(System.in)` then `sc.nextLine()`), so the origin lookup needs a\nhop through the receiver's binding -- WI-lalot's territory. Measured with a\nlive control (probes/java4): java.sql.ResultSet.getString -> Files.writeString\nVIOLATES, while Scanner-over-stdin and BufferedReader-over-stdin both produce\nnothing and Scanner-over-a-File correctly stays silent.\n\nTWO BLOCKERS FILED RATHER THAN WORKED AROUND, both found because a control was\ndead:\n  INV-hahak (P1) -- a java wildcard import binds every unqualified class name\n  to that wildcard's package AND overrides a fully-qualified call site, so\n  `Files.writeString` under `import java.io.*;` lands as java.io.Files and\n  matches no row. 2556 wildcard imports / 1622 files / 464 with two or more /\n  38 repos. This is why the first java probe had no SINK at all.\n  INV-fofoj (P1) -- a catalogued primitive whose call site is not a function\n  APPLICATION emits no matchable edge: haskell getLine/getChar/getContents/\n  readLn (27 sites, 8 repos) and java's System.in attribute, which is java's\n  ONLY ipc_recv row. So java has no reachable untrusted_input source through\n  ipc_recv at all, and INV-muvis's catalogue half being done for haskell did\n  not make those calls reachable.\n\nNeither blocker is a prerequisite for the java wrapper work in principle -- the\nwrapper fix would mint at Scanner.nextLine from the constructor's argument\ntext, which does not need System.in to be rowed -- but with both live, a java\nchange cannot be validated against a control the way c and haskell were, and\n\"shipped without a live control\" is the shape that cost this item a retraction\nonce already.\n\nNOT CLAIMED: fluent-bit is absent from the cohort (the 1-repo mini trial\nunderestimated it by more than 2x and it was killed at 30 minutes), so the\nc evidence rests on firejail and redis. java and the java blockers are\nunmeasured beyond the probes."  # cd0b
